rt64: draw only into the console's memory

RT64 draws a frame twice. Rendering to RAM -- on by default -- replays each
framebuffer pair at the console's own resolution and copies the result back into
RDRAM, which is what the RDP did and what a game that reads its own framebuffer
needs. Then the workload queue renders the frame again at the window's
resolution, and the present queue shows it.

A caller that only wants the frame out of the console's memory needs the first
and neither of the others, and the others are what hold it back. The workload
queue is a ring the render thread empties, and the present queue waits on the
display for a drawable, so a game stepped faster than the screen refreshes spends
its graphics thread spinning in `advanceToNextWorkload` for a slot: a hundred and
twenty agent steps a second, whatever the machine could do.

`ApplicationConfiguration::renderOnlyToRAM` renders to RAM whatever the emulator
configuration says, hands no workload to the render thread -- the same one is
reset and filled again by the next display list -- and presents nothing. The
swap chain is still made, on whatever window the application was given.

